format has no -i/--in-place flag. ArgumentParser does not recognise the
option, so md exits 64 with "Error: Unknown option '-i'" and input.md is left
exactly as it was.

What it should do: take -i the way replace, remove, insert-after and
insert-before all do — write the formatted document back over the file, print
nothing to stdout, and exit 0.

Cause: FormatCommand declares only `--frontmatter` and the shared InputOptions
group. It has no `@Flag(name: .shortAndLong) var inPlace`, and its run() always
calls InputReader.writeToStdout. Every other command that rewrites a document
has that flag, and InputReader.write already does the atomic staged write the
flag needs, so the parts are all there.

Why it matters more here than elsewhere: format is the command a user reaches
for to tidy a file they already have. Without -i the only way to do that is a
shell redirect back onto the same path, and the shell truncates the file
before md ever reads it, so the document is destroyed rather than formatted.

This case also pins the two halves of the behaviour: stdout must be empty, and
expected-file.md is the whole of what input.md should hold afterwards.
